  urldate = {2019-10-02},
  abstract = {Purpose: Identifying and understanding causal risk factors for crime over the life-course 8 9 is a key area of inquiry in developmental criminology. Prospective longitudinal studies provide 10 11 12 valuable information about the relationships between risk factors and later criminal offending. 13 14 Meta-analyses that synthesize findings from these studies can summarize the predictive strength 15 16 17 of different risk factors for crime, and offer unique opportunities for examining the 18 19 developmental variability of risk factors. Complex data structures are common in such meta20 21 analyses, whereby primary studies provide multiple (dependent) effect sizes. 22 23 24 Methods: This paper describes a recent innovative method for handling complex meta25 26 analytic data structures arising due to dependent effect sizes: robust variance estimation (RVE). 27 28 29 We first present a brief overview of the RVE method, describing the underlying models and 30 31 estimation procedures and their applicability to meta-analyses of research in developmental 32 33 34 criminology. We then present a tutorial on implementing these methods in the R statistical 35 36 environment, using an example meta-analysis on risk factors for adolescent delinquency. 37 38 Results: The tutorial demonstrates how to estimate mean effect sizes and meta-regression 39 40 41 models using the RVE method in R, with particular emphasis on exploring developmental 42 43 variation in risk factors for crime and delinquency. The tutorial also illustrates hypothesis testing 44 45 46 for meta-regression coefficients, including tests for overall model fit and incremental hypothesis 47 48 tests. 49 50 51 Conclusions: The paper concludes by summarizing the benefits of using the RVE method 52 53},
